WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.619
Connection strings often leak through operational surfaces rather than through obvious source code.

2
00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:20.458
Logs, exception messages, telemetry, distributed traces, crash dumps, SQL diagnostics, health checks, debug pages, configuration dumps, CI output, support screenshots, and ticket attachments all need review.

3
00:00:20.458 --> 00:00:29.885
Do not log full connection strings, passwords, access tokens, shared keys, SAS tokens, credential-bearing URLs, or raw configuration objects.

4
00:00:29.885 --> 00:00:34.766
Structured logging can make it easy to record entire objects by accident.

5
00:00:34.766 --> 00:00:40.917
Redaction should be deliberate and tested in the places where logs are produced and shipped.

6
00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:44.995
Redaction should preserve enough context for troubleshooting.

7
00:00:44.995 --> 00:00:53.285
Operators may need a connection name, provider, environment, safe endpoint label, database role category, or correlation ID.

8
00:00:53.285 --> 00:00:56.361
They do not need the credential-bearing value.

9
00:00:56.361 --> 00:01:00.974
Good redaction keeps the diagnostic signal while removing the secret.

10
00:01:00.974 --> 00:01:11.671
Error messages shown to users or clients should not reveal database hosts, usernames, provider details, internal network structure, or connection configuration.

11
00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:20.563
A public error should explain the user-facing problem, while protected logs capture safe operational categories for the support team.

12
00:01:20.563 --> 00:01:25.644
Support and incident workflows should include secret-redaction expectations.

13
00:01:25.644 --> 00:01:33.266
Screenshots, copied terminal output, health check payloads, and support bundles should be reviewed before sharing.

14
00:01:33.266 --> 00:01:38.281
A well-meaning support process can expose more than the application itself.

15
00:01:38.281 --> 00:01:53.123
A practical redaction review follows the connection string through the system: configuration load, dependency injection, database provider, exception handling, telemetry, pipeline logs, support tools, and incident records.

16
00:01:53.123 --> 00:01:57.268
Each surface should have an answer for what is safe to record.
